#limitation(title: [The Null Is Long COVID; the Positive Reports Are Mostly ME/CFS])[
The contradicting study and the supporting studies did not measure the same disease. This bounds how far the null travels, and it also bounds how far the positive reports travel.

The null cohort was specialist-confirmed Long COVID, never hospitalized, with anticoagulant and antiplatelet users excluded @Fraser2026LCcoagulation. The principal positive report was 25 patients meeting ME/CFS criteria @NunesPlatelets2022mecfs. The 80-patient series was a self-reporting Long COVID registry with mixed severity and no quantified comparison against its control samples @Pretorius2022LCmicroclotPrevalence. These are different case definitions applied to different populations, and the book's microclot claims are ME/CFS claims.

Two consequences follow, and they pull in opposite directions. First, the null constrains the *Long COVID extension* of the microclot claim more strongly than the ME/CFS core claim, so the ME/CFS findings are not directly refuted. Second, the book has been using Long COVID microclot findings to lend support to ME/CFS microclot claims, and that inferential bridge is now weaker in one direction: the best-controlled Long COVID study found nothing to extend.

If the full null held and microaggregates had no role anywhere, three things in this book would need revision: the claim in this section that microclot pathology is not unique to Long COVID, the Family 6 evidence status that rests partly on microclot findings, and any treatment inference drawn from microclot biology. The first two have been qualified in this cycle; the third is now argued against directly.

What would close the gap is specific: the Fraser assay — same blinded confocal quantification, same anticoagulant and antiplatelet exclusion — run on a cohort meeting a current ME/CFS case definition. Until that exists, the two literatures cannot be pooled.

*Consequence:* The ME/CFS microclot claim survives on its own evidence, but it can no longer borrow strength from Long COVID, and the Long COVID-to-ME/CFS extension should be written as an inference rather than as a finding. For a patient, this means a Long COVID study that found nothing does not overturn an ME/CFS finding, and an ME/CFS finding does not establish what happens in Long COVID. (Origin: brainstorm.)
